CFG226 POST HOC diagnostics (written after the main run, whose C1 slope control failed narrowly at 3.597 against the frozen window [3.6, 4.2]; reported only, not frozen):
where does the shallow plain-BTFR slope come from, and does the beta > 0 preference track it?  The plain power-law slope (same effective-variance likelihood) for the sample, its subsamples and an
inverse regression; the F1 beta_hat and the improvement over beta = 0 restricted to galaxies above a mass cut.  kappa = 1/2 FITTED; no law verdict.

plain BTFR slope (effective-variance fit of log M on log v, sigma_int free); inverse-regression slope beside it
  Upsilon 0.5, all   N = 121: slope 3.597 (sigma_int 0.149); inverse-regression slope 3.833
  Upsilon 0.35       N = 121: slope 3.436 (sigma_int 0.153); inverse-regression slope 3.688
  Upsilon 0.70       N = 121: slope 3.741 (sigma_int 0.149); inverse-regression slope 3.970
  gas-dominated      N =  44: slope 3.511 (sigma_int 0.158); inverse-regression slope 4.397

restricting to galaxies above a baryonic mass cut (Upsilon 0.5): plain slope, F1 beta_hat (kpc) and the improvement in -2 lnL over beta = 0
  log10 M_bar >=  0.0: N = 121, slope 3.597, beta_hat 0.262 kpc, a0 1.953e-10, improvement 18.78
  log10 M_bar >=  8.5: N = 119, slope 3.585, beta_hat 0.290 kpc, a0 1.980e-10, improvement 18.72
  log10 M_bar >=  9.0: N = 107, slope 3.510, beta_hat 0.471 kpc, a0 2.118e-10, improvement 17.26
  log10 M_bar >=  9.5: N =  81, slope 3.206, beta_hat 1.143 kpc, a0 2.585e-10, improvement 23.65
  log10 M_bar >= 10.0: N =  63, slope 2.841, beta_hat 2.603 kpc, a0 4.247e-10, improvement 21.49

Reading: the fitted beta tracks the plain slope; a slope below 4 has other causes (Upsilon, distances, inclination, pressure support in dwarfs), so a beta > 0 preference does not single out this model.
